#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1352" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}}$ given that $z \\lt 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}} & = \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt{y^2 z^3 \\cdot y^2 z^3} &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\sqrt{\\left(y^2 z^3\\right)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t|y^2 z^3| & = \\[5pt]\n|y^2| \\cdot |z^3| & = \\[5pt]\n|y^2| \\cdot |z|^3\n\t}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have $|y^2| = y^2.$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $z \\lt 0,$ and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|z| = -z.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}^{2}|\\cdot |z{|}^{3}={y}^{2}\\cdot (-z)^{3}=-{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1353" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}}$ given that $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r}{s} \\cdot \\dfrac{r}{s}} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right)^2} &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\left|\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right| &= \\[5pt]\n\n\\dfrac{|r|}{|s|} & \n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe are told that $s \\gt 0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|s| = s.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|r|}{|s|} = \\dfrac{|r|}{s} .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e876c5a-9037-5baf-acbf-9ba00705fd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4fgh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500564} {:answer/id #uuid "30583952-b0c6-55ee-864d-2722626ca01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2fg{h}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500567} {:answer/id #uuid "32d2d786-51d7-5f07-ab4d-ba30edce218c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2fgh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500574} {:answer/id #uuid "43664aa3-e124-5faa-aa77-ae09767124ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2fgh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500597} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8ae3d4-7dac-56f0-afcc-4269286bdf5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4fgh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32d2d786-51d7-5f07-ab4d-ba30edce218c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2fgh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500574} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c3eb9e-8664-5cd5-8345-3716f6532fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d271c02f-63d9-52da-abf5-d399868f3744" :question/math-academy-id "q-110919" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{4{f}^{2}{g}^{2}{h}^{2}}$ given that $f<0,$ $g<0$ and $h>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{f}^{2}{g}^{2}{h}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2fgh)}^{2}} & = \\\\ |2fgh| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |fgh| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |fgh| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $f<0,$ $g<0$ and $h>0.$ We know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |fgh| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |f|\\cdot |g|\\cdot |h| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot (-f)\\cdot (-g)\\cdot (h) & = \\\\ 2fgh.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14751749-11a7-54dd-a588-054ae0830a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500512} {:answer/id #uuid "1591c3c8-6452-5e01-9abd-248681176903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{y}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500514} {:answer/id #uuid "50955ec0-816e-58a7-a9d7-c62e54da9246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500632} {:answer/id #uuid "927ababd-3fe9-5c8c-9f72-29c5ebc246de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{6}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500770} {:answer/id #uuid "b341937b-e966-5703-8327-863c0e18b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b341937b-e966-5703-8327-863c0e18b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{4}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500842} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4993c43-1633-58f3-990c-54e890b3dad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ef216dc-f6cf-5481-bcc8-f1b9abb1bf84" :question/math-academy-id "q-2913" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}}$ given that $y>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{4}{y}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{4}{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({t}^{4}{y}^{3})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn even power of a number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have $|{t}^{4}|={t}^{4}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $y>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|y|=y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3}={t}^{4}\\cdot {y}^{3}={t}^{4}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178516} {:answer/id #uuid "414b8ab5-e58e-5e5f-afd8-5d24e4765f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178518} {:answer/id #uuid "5af6a186-f70b-53bc-a5e9-a363d3f26134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178520} {:answer/id #uuid "b31894e7-afeb-578b-a355-395a21451c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500841} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb7efd-f41c-5bbf-86ff-9b2b6e0d2516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178516} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d10b6a6-f98d-52cb-975a-842633722f73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec502873-fbf5-5e68-afb5-0d5690849cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2930" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}$ given that $a>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a|=a.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0863c307-3fc4-50bb-8193-2637b4d1fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500482} {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c7a4-9e6e-5b6f-85e7-5e7350d7fb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500565} {:answer/id #uuid "c0622a2f-34d6-58c4-b16a-d4b9c8c6f07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500866} {:answer/id #uuid "efca88fd-4fef-5d52-a234-b3f67f03da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19bc5229-73fa-5621-ae34-90fcabb4ab10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500522} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fd739d-61ce-572d-ba84-147f6daa016b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e44f5e7f-50c8-5f61-b0b8-699aaec6f842" :question/math-academy-id "q-52504" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ given that $y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{y})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |\\frac{3}{y}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|}{|y|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y<0.$ To take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|y|=-y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{|y|}=-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$"}]}
